This is the exercism forth exercise. The classic tricky test: "can define a word that uses a word with the same name" — `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;`, `foo` → 11. This means that when defining `foo` the second time, the `foo` inside the definition must refer to the old definition (lexical scoping at definition time). The standard approach: store definitions as a list of resolved tokens (tokens resolved against the dictionary at the time of definition). This handles it naturally: when defining `: foo foo 1 + ;`, the `foo` token is resolved against the current dictionary (the old foo), and the definition stores the resolved tokens.

Also "can use words with the same name in different ways": `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;`, `: foo 6 ;`, `bar foo` → 5 6. This also works with the resolve-at-definition-time approach.

But wait — there's a subtle point: if you store resolved tokens, then when a user word calls a built-in word, you store the built-in function. When a user word calls another user word, you store the resolved token list (or the function). The simplest approach: represent every word as a function func(*vm) error, and when defining, resolve each token at definition time to a function.

Note the spec: "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits, and a word is a sequence of one or more letters, digits, symbols, or punctuation that is not a number." So `-1` is a number (a negative number). Tokenization: split by spaces. A token is a number if it matches `^-?[0-9]+$`.

Definition syntax: `: word-name definition ;`. The word name cannot be a number. Definition: `:`, name, tokens..., `;`. If the name is a number, error ("cannot redefine a non-negative number").

